  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 PM EDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast: Impressive 600dM 500mb heat dome is \ncentered over the Southeast states this afternoon -- some +3 to 4 SD \nabove the climatological mean for mid-September. Meanwhile, fairly \nzonal flow exists across the north-central and northeast U.S., \nbecoming weakly troughed the farther north into Canada you go.\nOther feature to note is closed upper low pressure digging southward \nover the West Coast while a wave with tropical Pacific origins makes \nheadway around the base of that wave into the desert southwest. This \nlatter wave will become the focus this weekend as it ejects into the \nnorthern Plains by Friday, eventually making headway into the \nMidwest and Great Lakes region late Friday into Saturday -- Pacific \nmoisture aplenty while doing so.  All of this to follow a weak \nnorthern stream perturbation that's currently trekking across \nSaskatchewan/MT/WY, expected to swing across the western Great Lakes \nlater tonight into early Thursday.\n\nForecast Details: Weak wave mentioned above is expected to trek \nacross the region from west to east later tonight into early \nThursday morning. uptick in clouds associated with this, along with \nscattered/light rain shower chances -- primarily after 08z tonight \nthrough late Thursday morning. A more favorable environment exists \nacross WI, with less moisture/instability locally, so current trends \nstill point toward a gradual diminishing trend (both areal and \nintensity) as these showers make it into our area. \n\nPrimarily dry conditions return Thursday afternoon, with breaks in \nthe clouds becoming increasingly more common. This holds for much of \nFriday, although a gradual uptick in cloud cover through Friday \nafternoon is expected. High temperatures Thursday in the low-mid \n70s, cooler on Friday with daytime highs largely spanning the 60s.\n\nBy late Friday into Friday night, more potent wave is expected to be \nmaking progress across the upper MS Valley with initial upstream \nrain and thunderstorm chances approaching. Certainly signals for \nheavy rain potential, especially in convective elements, Friday \nnight through much of the weekend across the western Great Lakes \nregion, but lots of uncertainty still exists as to where that best \npotential lies. Lots of this uncertainty hinging on a stationary \nboundary that'll be draped to our south late this week, and how far \nnorth (if at all) that can be drawn. Current confidence is in a \nsolution that brings a swath of potentially efficient rain into \nparts of northern lower MI (primarily M-72 and south) Friday night \ninto early Saturday with additional heavy rain chances thereafter \nfocused to our south across southern WI/northern IL/northern IN and \nsouthern MI. This largely fits with WPC's latest long range QPF \nforecast and Day 4-5 EROs that focus highest probabilities of \npotentially impactful rain to our south. Certainly a timeframe \nthat'll be worth monitoring over the coming days.",
